<Layout
  title="Pickleball Core Thickness — 14 mm vs 16 mm vs 20 mm | PaddleFitHub"
  description="Understand the tradeoffs between 14 mm, 16 mm, and 20 mm pickleball paddle cores. Which thickness suits your play style?"
  canonicalPath="/blog/core-thickness-explained/"
>
  <div class="container-narrow py-16">
    <p class="text-caption-mono text-mute uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Resource</p>
    <h1 class="text-display-xl text-ink mb-4">Core Thickness Explained</h1>
    <p class="text-body-lg text-body mb-8">The core thickness of your paddle changes how it feels, sounds, and performs. Here is the breakdown.</p>

    <div class="prose text-body-md text-body space-y-6 max-w-none">
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">14 mm — Maximum pop</h2>
      <p>Thinnest core. Delivers the fastest ball speed off the face with the most audible pop. Least control and most vibration. Best for aggressive power players. Not ideal for arm sensitivity or noise-restricted venues.</p>

      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">16 mm — The sweet spot</h2>
      <p>The most popular thickness for good reason. Balanced power and control with a generous sweet spot and moderate noise. Works well for all playing styles. The safest choice if you are unsure.</p>

      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">20 mm — Maximum control</h2>
      <p>Thickest core. Softest feel, quietest sound, and largest sweet spot. Best for finesse players and those with arm concerns. Tradeoff is less power on drives — you will need to swing harder.</p>

      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Keep in mind</h2>
      <p>Core material matters too. Most paddles use polymer honeycomb, but face material (carbon fiber vs fiberglass) and edge construction also affect feel. Use the <a href="/tools/core-thickness-advisor/">Core Thickness Advisor</a> to see which thickness matches your priorities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-hairline">
      <a href="/tools/core-thickness-advisor/" class="btn-primary">Try the Core Thickness Advisor</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>